<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	The daemon's entitlements. Keep this list at exactly what the daemon needs
	to do its job as root. Scripts/package-deb.sh re-reads it out of the
	*signed* binary: a lost entitlement does not break the build, it makes the
	daemon refuse the app at runtime, which looks like a bug anywhere but
	where it is.

	`platform-application` is what lets an ad-hoc signed binary run as a
	platform binary at all. The two storage entitlements match the app's: what
	the app reads of another app's bundle or container, the daemon may be asked
	to open for it.

	Root and `no-sandbox` do not open a data vault. A process that writes
	under `/var/mobile/Library/Photos` (and friends) needs
	`com.apple.private.security.storage.<Class>` on *this* process. A sampler
	or a thin proxy does not.

	`wiki.qaq.irisin.client` is what irisind (Irisin 4.7 and later) asks of a
	client, beside `platform-application` and `no-sandbox`: the session host,
	which is this executable started with `--session`, installs, removes
	and resprings through it. It is eligibility, not identity — any
	installed package can carry it (Irisin's Documentation/ExternalClients.md).
	The app never carries it: installing is the host's alone.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.private.security.no-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.private.security.storage.AppBundles</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.private.security.storage.AppDataContainers</key>
	<true/>
	<key>platform-application</key>
	<true/>
	<key>wiki.qaq.irisin.client</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
